{# Epic 23 #03: one exit-node pair (tunnel + exit node + routing sidecar) from one exit_nodes entry
   (rendered per item — see docker-compose.yml.j2's docker_list_fragments loop, `item` is that entry:
   item.name / item.region / item.city). Dedicated network (exit_nodes_net, declared alongside the
   standard gateway/internal networks in docker-compose.yml.j2) — never gateway or internal — so a
   compromised container has no Docker-network path to Authelia or Hermes (epic 23 spec, trust
   boundary point 2). No host networking, no published ports anywhere in this fragment.

   Fixes ticket #01's live spike validated, applied here:
     1. Firewall backend: TS_DEBUG_FIREWALL_MODE=nftables + TS_USERSPACE=false (the fourth fix
        ticket #01 found — the stock image defaults to userspace networking, which has no kernel
        tailscale0 interface at all and makes exit-node function silently impossible).
     2. Forwarding: no manual iptables/nft rules — with the above two set, Tailscale installs its
        own ts-forward/ts-postrouting nftables chains automatically (ticket #01, confirmed live);
        gluetun's own default firewall needed no FIREWALL_* tuning either, matching the exact
        working spike config.
     3. Return path: the sidecar below reapplies the higher-priority ip rule on every one of ITS
        OWN starts, since it does not survive tunnel restart or recreation. -#}
{% macro recovery_healthcheck() -%}
{# Ticket #07's live attended validation found the real gap this closes: node and sidecar each
   share the tunnel's network namespace only as of THEIR OWN last (re)start — restarting the tunnel
   alone (a crash under its own restart policy, or a plain `docker restart`) leaves a still-running
   node/sidecar attached to the OLD, now-orphaned namespace indefinitely; `docker compose up -d`
   does not detect or fix this (the container ID never changed). See roles/exit_nodes/defaults/
   main.yml's own comment on exit_nodes_recovery_check_host for the full reasoning. This probes real
   TCP connectivity and self-terminates (kill -TERM its own PID 1) only after
   exit_nodes_recovery_check_fail_threshold CONSECUTIVE failures — never on one blip, so gluetun's
   own internal reconnect (seconds-long, self-resolving, no container restart) doesn't trigger a
   needless one — which restart: unless-stopped then picks up; a normal restart correctly
   re-resolves network_mode: service:tunnel to the tunnel's CURRENT namespace.

   Every literal `$` below is doubled (`$$`) — a real bug found deploying this fix live: Docker
   Compose does its OWN `$VAR`/`${VAR}` interpolation on compose-file content before the container
   ever sees it, so an unescaped `$n` or `$(cmd)` gets silently resolved against compose's variable
   namespace (where `n` is unset) and replaced with an empty string — `docker inspect` on the live
   container confirmed `echo $n > ...` had become `echo  > ...`. `$$` is Compose's documented escape
   for a literal `$`, restoring the shell semantics this script actually needs.

   The counter file is removed before `kill 1`, not just on a successful probe: `restart:
   unless-stopped` restarts the SAME container (its writable layer, and therefore /tmp, survives),
   so an unreset counter already at the threshold would make the very next single failed probe
   after a restart re-trigger `kill 1` immediately, rather than requiring fail_threshold fresh
   consecutive failures again. `retries` matches exit_nodes_recovery_check_fail_threshold (not a
   smaller hardcoded value) purely so Docker's own health status — visible via `docker ps`/`docker
   inspect` — flips to unhealthy in step with this script's own restart trigger, instead of
   mid-way through the debounce window; nothing here actually gates on that status.

   Cold-start note (an independent review of this fix raised it; not yet exercised live — ticket
   #07's own reboot test is still deferred): `start_period` only suppresses DOCKER's unhealthy-
   status *accounting* during that window, it does not stop the test command itself from running
   and incrementing the counter file from the very first probe. node/sidecar's `depends_on:
   condition: service_healthy` on the tunnel (below) exists specifically so a fresh `docker compose
   up -d` or a full host reboot doesn't start them racing a tunnel that's still negotiating its VPN
   handshake — they wait for the tunnel's own (image-provided) healthcheck first, so by the time
   their own debounce window starts, the tunnel is normally already reachable. #}
  healthcheck:
    test:
      - CMD-SHELL
      - >-
        nc -z -w3 {{ exit_nodes_recovery_check_host }} {{ exit_nodes_recovery_check_port }} 2>/dev/null &&
        { rm -f /tmp/hermes_recovery_fails; exit 0; };
        n=$$(( $$(cat /tmp/hermes_recovery_fails 2>/dev/null || echo 0) + 1 ));
        echo $$n > /tmp/hermes_recovery_fails;
        if [ $$n -ge {{ exit_nodes_recovery_check_fail_threshold }} ]; then rm -f /tmp/hermes_recovery_fails; kill 1; fi;
        exit 1
    interval: 30s
    timeout: 5s
    retries: {{ exit_nodes_recovery_check_fail_threshold }}
    start_period: 20s
{%- endmacro %}
{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-tunnel:
  image: "{{ exit_nodes_gluetun_image }}"
  container_name: "{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-tunnel"
  cap_add:
    - NET_ADMIN
  devices:
    - /dev/net/tun:/dev/net/tun
  networks:
    - exit_nodes_net
  env_file:
    - path: "{{ exit_nodes_credentials_file }}"
      required: false
  environment:
    VPN_SERVICE_PROVIDER: windscribe
    VPN_TYPE: wireguard
    SERVER_REGIONS: "{{ item.region }}"
    SERVER_CITIES: "{{ item.city }}"
    TZ: UTC
    UPDATER_PERIOD: "{{ exit_nodes_updater_period }}"
    UPDATER_VPN_SERVICE_PROVIDERS: "{{ exit_nodes_updater_vpn_service_providers }}"
  restart: unless-stopped

{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-node:
  image: "{{ exit_nodes_tailscale_image }}"
  container_name: "{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-node"
  network_mode: "service:{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-tunnel"
  cap_add:
    - NET_ADMIN
    - NET_RAW
  devices:
    - /dev/net/tun:/dev/net/tun
  env_file:
    - path: "{{ exit_nodes_credentials_file }}"
      required: false
  environment:
    TS_EXTRA_ARGS: "--advertise-exit-node --hostname={{ exit_nodes_hostname_prefix }}-ws-{{ item.name }} --reset"
    TS_STATE_DIR: /var/lib/tailscale
    TS_USERSPACE: "false"
    TS_DEBUG_FIREWALL_MODE: nftables
  volumes:
    - "{{ exit_nodes_state_dir }}/{{ item.name }}:/var/lib/tailscale"
  depends_on:
    "{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-tunnel":
      condition: service_healthy
  restart: unless-stopped
{{ recovery_healthcheck() }}

{# The routing sidecar (ticket #03's own contribution — ticket #01 applied this rule by hand during
   the spike, this fragment is what makes it self-healing for real). Reuses the already-pinned
   Tailscale image (it ships iproute2) instead of introducing a new image dependency purely for one
   shell command. `|| true` on each ip rule: idempotent-safe against a restart where the tunnel's
   netns (and therefore the rule) never actually changed, so re-adding would otherwise error on an
   already-present rule.
   Accepted, known tradeoff: `|| true` also swallows a genuine failure (a bad table, a missing
   capability, a future image losing iproute2) with the same silence as "rule already present" —
   the sidecar still reaches the wait loop below and looks healthy either way. No operator-visible
   signal distinguishes the two today; a real gap could go unnoticed until a client actually
   traces on the wrong route. Not fixed here since ticket #01 live-validated this exact command;
   revisit if a future gluetun/Tailscale bump ever changes iproute2's exit codes or availability.

   No `exec sleep infinity` (ticket #07's live attended validation found why): a plain `sleep`
   running AS PID 1 installs no signal handler of its own, and the kernel makes PID 1 of a PID
   namespace immune to any UNHANDLED signal — including SIGKILL — sent by a process INSIDE that
   same namespace (pid_namespaces(7); confirmed live: `docker exec ... kill -9 1` did nothing, only
   an external `docker kill` from the host succeeded). The shared recovery_healthcheck() macro below
   signals PID 1 from inside the container, so it needs PID 1 to have an explicit handler — `trap`
   installs one, which makes the kernel treat the signal as handled rather than exempt, and
   `wait $!` on a backgrounded sleep (rather than a blocking `sleep` builtin) wakes immediately when
   that trap fires. Tailscale's own containerboot (the node service's PID 1) already traps SIGTERM
   this way, which is why ticket #07 observed node recover on the first try while sidecar did not —
   this is what brings sidecar to the same behavior. #}
{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-sidecar:
  image: "{{ exit_nodes_tailscale_image }}"
  container_name: "{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-sidecar"
  network_mode: "service:{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-tunnel"
  cap_add:
    - NET_ADMIN
  entrypoint: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
  command:
    - >-
      trap 'exit 0' TERM;
      ip rule add to {{ exit_nodes_tailnet_subnet_v4 }} lookup {{ exit_nodes_return_route_table }}
      priority {{ exit_nodes_return_route_priority }} || true;
      ip -6 rule add to {{ exit_nodes_tailnet_subnet_v6 }} lookup {{ exit_nodes_return_route_table }}
      priority {{ exit_nodes_return_route_priority }} || true;
      while true; do sleep 3600 & wait $$!; done
  depends_on:
    "{{ exit_nodes_service_prefix }}-{{ item.name }}-tunnel":
      condition: service_healthy
  restart: unless-stopped
{{ recovery_healthcheck() }}
